  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Exploring Tailwind v4**\n\nI\'m now investigating Tailwind CSS v4\'s support for custom colors and font families within the `@theme` directive. So far, I\'ve confirmed that the `@theme` block allows for defining custom CSS variables, like `--color-custom-bg` and `--color-custom-card-bg`. I\'m curious to see how these variables integrate into the rest of the styling system.\n\n\n**Defining Custom Variables**\n\nI\'ve been defining a suite of custom color variables within the theme. I\'ve set colors for dividers, sidebars, panels, muted text, and buttons, along with a few primary, secondary, and tertiary colors. I\'m also now adding Google Fonts, like Inter, JetBrains Mono, and Manrope, which I plan to incorporate directly into the styles. I can import these using `<link>` tags.\n\n\n**Structuring the Romer App**\n\nNow I\'m diving into the application\'s structure, starting with the Romer landing page. I\'m focusing on perfectly replicating the designs from the images, including the fixed header with the logo, navigation, and action buttons. The hero section\'s heading and subhead typography, using Manrope and tracking, are also on my radar. I also want to determine the proper Material Symbols font integration from `Image_2_0f028922.html`.\n\n\n**Designing Interactive Components**\n\nI\'m now sketching out interactive elements for the Technical Dashboard. I\'ve defined button actions: \"Get Started\" for workspace setup and \"Book a Demo\" for scheduling. I\'m visualizing the live link and a mock-up of the dashboard header, with animated data streams in the SVG canvas, including interactive hover elements and minimal floating cards.\n\n\n**Visualizing Data Structures**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a unified visual approach. I\'m exploring rectangles and layers within a unified view (FIG 0.1), employing coordinate inspection and a click-to-toggle layer highlight. I\'m also examining a decision graph (FIG 0.2) with a concentric node network, interactive pinging of the ROOT_V1 node, and an automated rhythm (FIG 0.3) using a sine wave with SYNC_EVENT [100, 100] and a live frequency scrubber.\n\n\n**Constructing Interactive Insights**\n\nI\'m now implementing interactive components for operational insights. I\'m focusing on a section that displays nodes linked by a divider, representing approval cycles, capacity forecasts, risk movement, and operating rhythm. Clicking on each node will open detailed operational insights, including graphs, SLA reduction data, and team calibration information. Additionally, I\'m integrating customer quotes in a light cyan and lime color scheme.\n\n\n**Building the Operational Interface**\n\nI\'m now creating the final calls to action, including \"Start Free Trial\" and \"Contact Sales\" buttons, and a categorized footer. I\'m focusing on the \"Live Operating Layer,\" which will be accessible from the \"Dashboards\" view, including a top bar with workspace breadcrumbs, latency, a time-range selector, and a quick search command palette. I\'m also planning the tabs with the executive overview and a signals & telemetry feed.\n\n\n**Defining Operational Components**\n\nI\'m now integrating aggregated signals from various platforms like GitHub, Jira, and Slack, filtering them by severity and team. I\'m also building decision logic with an execution board for leadership, including an audit log. I\'m visualizing the operating rhythm with a milestone scrubber and bottleneck heatmaps. I\'m adding interactive actions like simulating anomalies and inspecting nodes. Lastly, I\'m constructing a \"Back to Platform\" toggle to return to the landing presentation.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
