    end_line: 387
    content: "---\nname: science\ndescription: >-\n  Comprehensive guide for scientific computing, bioinformatics, genomics, molecular biology,\n  chemistry, and biomedical literature in AI Studio applications. Use when querying\n  protein structures, genetic variants, chemical compounds, biological pathways, ontologies,\n  scholarly literature, clinical trials, or rendering 3D molecular structures.\n---\n\n# Science Skills\n\nThis skill provides the suite of Google DeepMind Science Skills adapted for\nbuilding interactive scientific web applications, bioinformatics tools, genomics\ndashboards, drug discovery scouts, and molecular visualizers in AI Studio.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] **MANDATORY REFERENCE LOADING**\n>\n> Before writing any bioinformatics, genomics, chemistry, pathway, literature,\n> or molecular visualization code, you **MUST** identify the relevant skill(s)\n> below and read the corresponding reference file(s) using `view_file` (for\n> example,\n> `/skills/system_skills/science/references/alphafold_database_fetch_and_analyze.md`\n> or `/skills/system_skills/science/references/proteins-and-structures.md`). Do\n> NOT rely on generic assumptions without reading the specific reference file(s)\n> first.\n\n## Domain Synthesis & Web Visualization Guides\n\n-   **[Proteins & Structures](references/proteins-and-structures.md)**: Unified\n    TypeScript/Web quickstart across AlphaFold DB, RCSB PDB, UniProtKB,\n    InterPro, Foldseek, and Clustal Omega MSA.\n-   **[Genomics & Variants](references/genomics-and-variants.md)**: Unified\n    TypeScript/Web quickstart across AlphaGenome Atlas links, ClinVar, gnomAD,\n    dbSNP, Ensembl VEP, ENCODE cCREs, JASPAR, UniBind, and UCSC conservation.\n-   **[Chemistry & Drugs](references/chemistry-and-drug-discovery.md)**: Unified\n    TypeScript/Web quickstart across PubChem, ChEMBL, Open Targets, openFDA, and\n    Lipinski/Veber drug-likeness rules.\n-   **[Pathways & Networks](references/pathways-and-networks.md)**: Unified\n    TypeScript/Web quickstart across Reactome, STRING, QuickGO, GTEx, and Human\n    Protein Atlas.\n-   **[Literature & Trials](references/literature-and-citations.md)**: Unified\n    TypeScript/Web quickstart across PubMed, Europe PMC, OpenAlex, arXiv,\n    bioRxiv, and ClinicalTrials.gov.\n-   **[Molecular Web 3D](references/molecular-visualization-web.md)**:\n    Interactive 3D molecular graphics with 3Dmol.js (cartoon, sticks, spheres,\n    surfaces, AlphaFold pLDDT color scale, ligand pocket highlighting) and 2D\n    SMILES chemical rendering with SmilesDrawer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## Catalog of Adapted Science Skills (32 Database & Analysis Skills + 2 Supporting Guides)\n\n### 1. Proteins, Sequences & Structural Biology\n\n-   **[AlphaFold DB](references/alphafold_database_fetch_and_analyze.md)**:\n    Fetch AlphaFold DB structure predictions (`cifUrl`, `bcifUrl`, `pdbUrl`),\n    per-residue pLDDT confidence scores, and Predicted Aligned Error (PAE)\n    domain matrices (`paeDocUrl`).\n-   **[PDB Database](references/pdb_database.md)**: Search experimental 3D\n    structures via RCSB Search v2 API, query metadata via RCSB Data GraphQL/REST\n    API, and download mmCIF/BinaryCIF coordinate files.\n-   **[UniProt Database](references/uniprot_database.md)**: Query UniProtKB\n    protein sequences, functional domains, active/binding sites, disease\n    variants, ID mapping (`idmapping`), and SPARQL queries.\n-   **[InterPro Database](references/interpro_database.md)**: Retrieve protein\n    families, homologous superfamilies, and domain architectures across Pfam,\n    SMART, PROSITE, CDD, and PANTHER.\n-   **[Foldseek Search](references/foldseek_structural_search.md)**: Perform\n    fast 3D structural similarity and alignment searches (`3diaa` and `tmalign`)\n    against AlphaFold DB and PDB via the Foldseek REST API.\n-   **[Sequence Search](references/protein_sequence_similarity_search.md)**:\n    Find homologous protein sequences using EMBL-EBI NCBI BLAST (`blastp`\n    against UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot) or ColabFold MMseqs2.\n-   **[Protein MSA](references/protein_sequence_msa.md)**: Compute multiple\n    sequence alignments (MSA), percent identity matrices, and guide trees via\n    the EMBL-EBI Clustal Omega REST API.\n-   **[NCBI Sequences](references/ncbi_sequence_fetch.md)**: Retrieve nucleotide\n    and protein sequences from NCBI Entrez (`efetch`, `esearch`, `elink`,\n    `esummary`), translate CDS features, and look up RefSeq, locus,\n    PubMed-linked, and patent sequences.\n\n### 2. Genomics, Variants & Regulatory Elements\n\n-   **[AlphaGenome Links](references/alphagenome_atlas_website_links.md)**:\n    Construct validated deep-links (`variant`, `locus`, and `table` modes) to\n    the AlphaGenome Atlas web application\n    (`https://deepmind.google.com/science/alphagenome/atlas?q=...`) using\n    `URLSearchParams` in TypeScript.\n-   **[ClinVar Database](references/clinvar_database.md)**: Query clinical\n    significance, ACMG classification, review status stars, and linked condition\n    phenotypes via NCBI ClinVar E-utilities.\n-   **[gnomAD Database](references/gnomad_database.md)**: Query population\n    allele frequencies (`ac`, `an`, `af`, `homozygote_count`) and gene\n    constraint metrics (`pli`, `oe_lof`, `oe_lof_lower`, `oe_lof_upper` / LOEUF,\n    `mis_z`, `syn_z`) via the gnomAD GraphQL API.\n-   **[dbSNP Database](references/dbsnp_database.md)**: Resolve rsIDs, HGVS\n    expressions, genomic coordinates, and SPDI notation via NCBI Variation\n    Services (via `server.ts` `/api/*` proxy) and E-utilities.\n-   **[Ensembl Database](references/ensembl_database.md)**: Query gene models,\n    MANE Select canonical transcripts, genomic sequences, cross-references, and\n    Variant Effect Predictor (VEP) annotations via the Ensembl REST API.\n-   **[ENCODE cCREs](references/encode_ccres_database.md)**: Query candidate\n    Cis-Regulatory Elements (promoters, proximal/distal enhancers, CTCF sites),\n    DNase/H3K4me3/H3K27ac Z-scores, and linked genes via the ENCODE SCREEN\n    GraphQL API.\n-   **[JASPAR Database](references/jaspar_database.md)**: Retrieve transcription\n    factor binding profiles, Position Frequency Matrices (PFMs), sequence logo\n    weights, and motif inference via the JASPAR REST API (proxied through\n    `server.ts`).\n-   **[UniBind Database](references/unibind_database.md)**: Query experimentally\n    validated direct transcription factor-DNA interaction sites (ChIP-seq TFBS)\n    across cell types and species via the UniBind REST API (proxied through\n    `server.ts`).\n-   **[UCSC Conservation](references/ucsc_conservation_and_tfbs.md)**: Fetch\n    base-by-base evolutionary conservation scores (`phyloP100way`,\n    `phastCons100way`) and transcription factor binding tracks via the UCSC\n    Genome Browser REST API.\n\n### 3. Chemistry, Pharmacology & Drug Discovery\n\n-   **[PubChem Database](references/pubchem_database.md)**: Query chemical\n    structures, CIDs, SMILES, InChI, physicochemical properties, 2D depictions,\n    3D SDF conformers, similarity searches, and GHS safety data via PUG REST and\n    PUG View.\n-   **[ChEMBL Database](references/chembl_database.md)**: Retrieve small\n    molecule and biologic bioactivities (`IC50`, `Ki`, `Kd`, `EC50`,\n    `pchembl_value`), target bindings, mechanisms of action, and clinical phases\n    via the ChEMBL REST API.\n-   **[Open Targets](references/opentargets_database.md)**: Query target-disease\n    evidence associations, target tractability (`label`, `modality`, `value`),\n    and clinical candidates (`drugAndClinicalCandidates`) via the Open Targets\n    Platform v4 GraphQL API.\n-   **[openFDA Database](references/openfda_database.md)**: Query FDA drug\n    adverse events (FAERS), drug product labeling (SPL boxed warnings,\n    indications, interactions), NDC directory, recalls, and approvals via the\n    openFDA API.\n\n### 4. Pathways, Networks, Ontologies & Expression\n\n-   **[Reactome Database](references/reactome_database.md)**: Query curated\n    human biological pathways, reaction hierarchies, participating molecules,\n    and pathway over-representation enrichment (`POST\n    https://reactome.org/AnalysisService/identifiers/projection`) via Reactome\n    Content and Analysis Services.\n-   **[STRING Database](references/string_database.md)**: Retrieve\n    protein-protein interaction (PPI) networks, evidence channel scores,\n    interaction partners, and functional enrichment via the STRING REST API.\n-   **[QuickGO Database](references/quickgo_database.md)**: Query Gene Ontology\n    (GO) terms (Molecular Function, Biological Process, Cellular Component),\n    ontology hierarchies, ECO evidence codes, and protein annotations via\n    EMBL-EBI QuickGO.\n-   **[EMBL-EBI OLS](references/embl_ebi_ols.md)**: Search terms, definitions,\n    synonyms, and parent/child hierarchies across 250+ biomedical ontologies\n    (GO, EFO, MONDO, HP, ChEBI, Uberon, DOID) via the OLS4 REST API.\n-   **[GTEx Database](references/gtex_database.md)**: Query baseline median gene\n    expression (TPM) across 54 healthy human tissues (`datasetId=gtex_v10`,\n    `gencodeVersion=v39`) and single-tissue eQTL associations via the GTEx\n    Portal API v2.\n-   **[Human Protein Atlas](references/human_protein_atlas_database.md)**: Query\n    tissue RNA/protein expression specificity, subcellular localization,\n    pathology prognostic markers, and druggability classes via the HPA API.\n\n### 5. Biomedical Literature, Preprints & Clinical Trials\n\n-   **[PubMed Database](references/pubmed_database.md)**: Search biomedical\n    literature, resolve citations, fetch abstracts and MeSH terms, and traverse\n    citation networks via NCBI E-utilities and PMC BioC JSON (proxied via\n    `server.ts` for `pmcoa.cgi`).\n-   **[Europe PMC](references/literature_search_europepmc.md)**: Search\n    peer-reviewed articles and preprints, retrieve open-access full-text XML,\n    and query citation/reference graphs via the Europe PMC REST API.\n-   **[OpenAlex](references/literature_search_openalex.md)**: Query global\n    scholarly works, citation counts, open-access status, authors, institutions,\n    funders, and hierarchical research topics via the OpenAlex API.\n-   **[arXiv Search](references/literature_search_arxiv.md)**: Search preprints\n    across quantitative biology (`q-bio`), computer science, physics, and\n    mathematics via the arXiv Atom API (proxied via `server.ts`).\n-   **[bioRxiv & medRxiv](references/literature_search_biorxiv.md)**: Query\n    life-science (`biorxiv`) and clinical (`medrxiv`) preprints by DOI or\n    publication date intervals and subject categories via `api.biorxiv.org`.\n-   **[ClinicalTrials.gov](references/clinical_trials_database.md)**: Search\n    clinical trial protocols, eligibility criteria, recruitment status, study\n    arms, and outcome measures via the ClinicalTrials.gov REST API v2.\n\n### 6. Scientific Credentials & Rate-Limit Configuration\n\n-   **[Credentials](references/credentials.md)**: Triggering AI Studio Build\'s\n    Secrets modal via `.env.example`, managing secrets via **Settings** ->\n    **Secrets**, safe server-side environment variable handling (`process.env`\n    in `server.ts`), and registration URLs for connector keys\n    (`OPENALEX_API_KEY`, `NCBI_API_KEY`, `FDA_API_KEY`, `USER_EMAIL`).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## API Keys & Safe Credentials Protocol (`environment.md` Compliant)\n\nAll 32 public scientific databases in this skill work out-of-the-box without an\nAPI key for standard queries. A subset of connectors support or require an API\nkey or polite email credential when running high-volume queries, authenticated\nendpoints, or asynchronous job submissions. Whenever an API key or credential is\nneeded (or requested for higher rate limits), read `references/credentials.md`\nand follow AI Studio\'s `environment.md` rule (**Never create custom client-side\nUI for API keys; use server-side proxy routes (`/api/*`)** and never ask the\nuser to paste raw API keys into the chat):\n\n1.  **Connectors Supporting or Requiring Credentials**:\n    -   **OpenAlex (`OPENALEX_API_KEY`)**: Standard REST search and works\n        endpoints (`https://api.openalex.org/works`) work without an API key;\n        authenticated or high-volume queries require `OPENALEX_API_KEY` from\n        `https://openalex.org/` (under Account Settings).\n    -   **ClinVar, PubMed, dbSNP, NCBI Sequence Fetch (`NCBI_API_KEY`)**:\n        Functional without a key at 3 requests/second; configuring\n        `NCBI_API_KEY` unlocks 10 requests/second. Obtain a free key at\n        `https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/account/settings/`.\n    -   **openFDA (`FDA_API_KEY`)**: Functional without a key at 1,000\n        requests/day (240 requests/minute); configuring `FDA_API_KEY` unlocks\n        120,000 requests/day. Obtain a free key at\n        `https://open.fda.gov/apis/authentication/`.\n    -   **EMBL-EBI Clustal Omega & NCBI BLAST (`USER_EMAIL`)**: Required by EBI\n        Job Dispatcher (`protein_sequence_msa`,\n        `protein_sequence_similarity_search`) for asynchronous job submission,\n        and recommended for OpenAlex (`?mailto=`) and NCBI polite headers. Read\n        `process.env.USER_EMAIL` in `server.ts` (falling back to a polite\n        placeholder such as `scientist@example.com` if unset).\n2.  **A) Trigger the AI Studio Build Secrets Modal via `.env.example`**:\n    -   AI Studio Build scans `.env.example` for uncommented `KEY_NAME=` lines\n        that are not yet stored in the applet\'s Secrets store and automatically\n        opens the **\"Enter your environment variable to continue\"** Secrets\n        modal in the chat dialog.\n    -   **When an API key is needed** (for example, the connector requires a\n        key, the user requests higher rate limits, or an upstream call returns\n        HTTP `401` / `429`), add an **uncommented** `KEY_NAME=` line (such as\n        `OPENALEX_API_KEY=`, `NCBI_API_KEY=`, `FDA_API_KEY=`, or `USER_EMAIL=`)\n        to `.env.example` and read `process.env.KEY_NAME` inside Express\n        `/api/*` proxy routes in `server.ts`. This immediately triggers the\n        Secrets modal in the chat dialog.\n    -   **When an API key is optional and not yet needed**, keep optional\n        rate-limit keys commented (`# NCBI_API_KEY=`, `# FDA_API_KEY=`) in\n        `.env.example` so the app runs immediately at public rate limits without\n        blocking on the modal, or uncomment the line whenever the user wants to\n        configure the key.\n3.  **B) Instruct the User on Both the Dialog Secrets Modal and `Settings` ->\n    `Secrets` Tab**:\n\n    -   Whenever an API key is needed (or optional for higher rate limits), your\n        chat response **MUST** include clear setup instructions covering both\n        entry points:\n\n        > **To configure your `<KEY_NAME>` API key:**\n        >\n        > 1.  **Get an API key**: Obtain your key from `<official_url>`.\n        > 2.  **Using the Secrets modal in the chat dialog**: When the **\"Enter\n        >     your environment variable to continue\"** prompt appears in the\n        >     chat, paste your key into the **Value** field for `<KEY_NAME>` and\n        >     click **Apply** (or press **Enter**). The app will save the secret\n        >     and rebuild automatically.\n        > 3.  **Using the Settings panel at any time**: Open **Settings** (the\n        >     gear icon in the top-right corner of AI Studio Build) -> select\n        >     the **Secrets** tab -> click **Add secret** (or edit `<KEY_NAME>`)\n        >     -> enter `<KEY_NAME>` and paste your key into **Value** -> click\n        >     **Apply changes** (or press **Enter**).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## TypeScript Retry & Rate-Limit Helper (`fetchWithRetry`)\n\nWhen querying rate-limited scientific REST or GraphQL APIs from TypeScript (in\n`server.ts` proxy routes or browser modules), use this exponential backoff\nhelper to handle HTTP `429 Too Many Requests` and transient `5xx` responses:\n\n```typescript\nexport async function fetchWithRetry(\n  url: string,\n  options: RequestInit = {},\n  maxRetries = 3,\n  baseDelayMs = 600\n): Promise<Response> {\n  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= maxRetries; attempt++) {\n    const response = await fetch(url, options);\n    if (response.ok) {\n      return response;\n    }\n    if (\n      (response.status === 429 || response.status >= 500) &&\n      attempt < maxRetries\n    ) {\n      const retryAfter = Number(response.headers.get(\'Retry-After\'));\n      const delay =\n        !Number.isNaN(retryAfter) && retryAfter > 0\n          ? retryAfter * 1000\n          : baseDelayMs * Math.pow(2, attempt);\n      await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, delay));\n      continue;\n    }\n    const errorBody = await response.text().catch(() => \'\');\n    throw new Error(\n      `HTTP ${response.status} (${response.statusText}) from ${url}: ${errorBody.slice(0, 300)}`\n    );\n  }\n  throw new Error(`Exhausted retries for ${url}`);\n}\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## Scientific UI & Palette Design System\n\nWhen building scientific web applications, dashboards, and molecular or genomic\nworkbenches in AI Studio, follow this **Scientific UI & Palette Design System**\nby default (unless the user explicitly requests a different visual theme):\n\n1.  **Clean, High-Contrast \"Scientific Workbench / Publication-Grade\" Light\n    Palette** (avoid generic neon or cyberpunk dark-mode AI templates):\n    -   **Backgrounds & Surfaces**: Use crisp laboratory light surfaces -\n        primary canvas `#F8FAFC` (`#F8F9FA`) and card surfaces `#FFFFFF` with\n        subtle `1px solid #E2E8F0` (`#DADCE0`) borders and minimal or zero drop\n        shadows. (Dark `#0F172A` viewports may still be used inside bounded 3D\n        WebGL `3Dmol.js` canvases for structural contrast when appropriate,\n        paired with a light/dark canvas toggle.)\n    -   **Typography**: Pair clean sans-serif UI headers and body text (`Inter`\n        or `Google Sans`) with monospace (`JetBrains Mono` or `Roboto Mono`) for\n        accessions, amino acid or nucleotide sequences, genomic coordinates,\n        SMILES strings, rsIDs, IC50 values, and p-values.\n    -   **Domain, Confidence & Semantic Color Encoding**:\n        -   **Primary Accent**: Google/DeepMind Scientific Blue (`#1A73E8` /\n            `#0B57D0`) and Teal (`#0D9488` / `#007B83`) for primary actions,\n            interactive links, and active controls.\n        -   **Multi-Series Chart Comparison**: Muted, colorblind-safe secondary\n            hues - Orange (`#E8710A`), Purple (`#9334E6`), and Teal (`#007B83`)\n            -   for comparing multiple targets, tissues, or compounds.\n        -   **AlphaFold pLDDT Confidence Scale**: Always use the canonical\n            4-band color scale - Very High `>90` (`#0053D6` deep blue),\n            Confident `70-90` (`#65CBF3` light blue), Low `50-70` (`#FFDB13`\n            yellow), and Very Low `<50` (`#FF7D45` orange).\n        -   **Clinical & Variant Pathogenicity**: Semantic status pills -\n            Pathogenic / Likely Pathogenic (`#DC2626` / `#D93025` crimson),\n            Uncertain Significance / VUS (`#D97706` amber), and Benign / Likely\n            Benign (`#059669` / `#1E8E3E` emerald).\n2.  **Enforce a Multi-Pane Scientific Workbench Layout**:\n    -   **Top Quick-Try Example Pill Bar**: Always include pre-loaded, clickable\n        example chips for real biological or chemical identifiers (for example,\n        `TP53`, `BRCA1`, `EGFR`, `PCSK9`, `P04637`, or `Imatinib`) at the top of\n        the workspace and auto-load a default example on initial mount so the\n        user never lands on an empty state.\n    -   **Split Interactive Canvas + Synchronized Data Inspector**: Pair the\n        interactive 3D molecular viewer (`3Dmol.js`), 2D chemical structure\n        canvas (`SmilesDrawer`), pathway network (`D3`), or quantitative chart\n        (`Chart.js` / `D3` with wheel-zoom suppressed and explicit Zoom In /\n        Zoom Out / Reset buttons) with a synchronized tabular/metadata inspector\n        where clicking a variant, domain, or compound highlights it in the\n        canvas.\n    -   **Epistemic Provenance & Source Citation Badges**: Display explicit\n        provenance badges (`[Experimentally determined]`, `[Predicted by\n        model]`, `[Inferred from homology]`, or `[Literature curated]`) and\n        direct outbound links to the authoritative source record (UniProt,\n        AlphaFold DB, RCSB PDB, ClinVar, gnomAD, OpenTargets, ChEMBL, PubChem,\n        STRING, Reactome, or Europe PMC / PubMed) on every data card.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## Core Principles & Best Practices\n\n1.  **Scientific Accuracy & Data Provenance**:\n    -   Never hallucinate or synthesize fake UniProt accession numbers, PDB IDs,\n        rsIDs, NCT IDs, or chemical SMILES strings.\n    -   Always query the authoritative public REST/GraphQL APIs or guide the\n        user to input valid identifiers.\n    -   Clearly distinguish experimental measurements (for example, RCSB PDB\n        crystal/cryo-EM structures) from computational predictions (for example,\n        AlphaFold DB structures).\n2.  **AlphaFold pLDDT Confidence Communication**:\n    -   Always surface the 4-tier pLDDT confidence scale in molecular\n        visualizers: Very High (>90, `#0053D6`), Confident (70-90, `#65CBF3`),\n        Low (50-70, `#FFDB13`), and Very Low (<50, `#FF7D45`).\n3.  **Modern Coordinate Formats (mmCIF Preferred)**:\n    -   Prefer mmCIF (`.cif`) or BinaryCIF (`.bcif`) over legacy `.pdb` format,\n        and always resolve AlphaFold DB file URLs dynamically via `GET\n        https://alphafold.ebi.ac.uk/api/prediction/{uniprot_accession}` (which\n        currently serves `_v6` coordinate and PAE files).\n4.  **Adherence to Public API Etiquette, CORS Proxies & Safe Credentials**:\n    -   Respect rate limits (NCBI E-utilities <= 3 req/sec without key or <= 10\n        req/sec with `NCBI_API_KEY`, Ensembl <= 15 req/sec, PubChem <= 5\n        req/sec, gnomAD <= 10 req/min) and cache responses in state during\n        interactive exploration.\n    -   Route non-CORS upstream endpoints (JASPAR, UniBind, arXiv Atom XML,\n        dbSNP `/variation/v0/refsnp/`, and PubMed Central `pmcoa.cgi` BioC JSON)\n        through Express `/api/*` routes in `server.ts`.\n"
    num_lines: 388
    num_bytes: 22410
